AUTHORS = [
 (17,'drug_medication','A formulated product containing a drug plus excipients is a {{c1::medication}}.','The drug is the active substance; the formulation determines how it is delivered.',3,[1565120487647,1489344367311]),
 (21,'adme','Absorption, distribution, metabolism and excretion together describe {{c1::pharmacokinetics}}.','Absorption gets drug into blood; distribution reaches tissues; metabolism transforms it; excretion removes it.',3,[1565120487647,1485913667420]),
 (23,'bioavailability','The fraction of an administered dose that reaches systemic circulation unchanged is {{c1::bioavailability}}.','An intravenous dose has complete systemic availability. Oral drug may be lost to incomplete absorption or first-pass metabolism.',3,[1565120487647,1472747814325]),
 (25,'targets','The four principal protein target families are receptors, enzymes, ion channels and {{c1::transporters}}.','Drugs can also act without a protein target, for example by osmotic or chemical effects.',3,[1461866207939,1472487477564,1484950518844]),
 (35,'target_response','A tissue effect produced downstream of drug–target signaling is the {{c1::clinical response}}.','The same target may be present in multiple tissues, so a drug can have intended and adverse effects.',3,[1522203660054,1472747773497]),
 (36,'rational_design','Rational drug design exploits structural and chemical {{c1::complementarity}} between a molecule and its target.','Shape, charge and hydrogen bonding favor recognition; a covalent bond can make inhibition persistent.',4,[1472426609949,1475982728516]),
 (33,'antacid','Antacids reduce gastric acidity by {{c1::chemical neutralization}} in the lumen.','A proton-pump inhibitor instead suppresses new acid secretion by blocking H+/K+-ATPase.',4,[1461624498591,1464478447015]),
 (37,'stereo','Enantiomers can differ in receptor binding because the target is {{c1::stereoselective}}.','They have the same molecular formula but a different three-dimensional arrangement. Thalidomide enantiomers interconvert in vivo.',4,[1461986072634,1484950587301]),
 (38,'orthosteric','The site occupied by the endogenous ligand is the {{c1::orthosteric}} site.','An allosteric ligand binds at another site and changes affinity or efficacy of the orthosteric ligand.',3,[1484768430296,1494990914793]),
 (38,'negative_allosteric','An allosteric modulator that weakens the response to an endogenous agonist is a {{c1::negative}} allosteric modulator.','A positive modulator enhances the response. Both bind outside the orthosteric site.',3,[1484768430296,1484768435794]),
 (39,'kd_vs_ec50','The concentration giving half-maximal drug effect is {{c1::EC50}}, not necessarily Kd.','Affinity and potency can diverge because signaling amplification and spare receptors affect response.',3,[1490066293330,1461852748718]),
 (40,'spare_receptors','When maximal response occurs before every receptor is occupied, the system has {{c1::spare receptors}}.','This explains why EC50 need not equal Kd and why initial irreversible receptor blockade may leave Emax intact.',3,[1490066293330,1490066311609]),
 (42,'inverse','A ligand that reduces constitutive receptor activity below baseline is an {{c1::inverse agonist}}.','A neutral antagonist occupies the receptor without shifting basal activity; it blocks agonist binding.',3,[1490066276769,1490066286562,1484768629105]),
 (50,'partial_compete','In the presence of a full agonist, a high-affinity partial agonist can {{c1::reduce}} total receptor response.','It displaces the full agonist while still providing some signaling; this can precipitate withdrawal.',3,[1484950383414,1484950415236]),
 (51,'biased','An agonist that preferentially activates one signaling route through the same receptor is a {{c1::biased agonist}}.','GPCRs can signal through G proteins and β-arrestin. Biased signaling does not guarantee absence of adverse effects.',4,[1522203660054,1484768590208]),
 (53,'physiological_antagonism','Two drugs acting at different targets can oppose each other at the tissue level through {{c1::physiological antagonism}}.','For example, insulin and glucagon exert opposite effects on blood glucose without competing for one receptor.',4,[1490066300391,1462128028823]),
 (54,'noncomp','An insurmountable antagonist typically lowers agonist {{c1::Emax}}.','Reversible competitive blockade instead shifts the curve right with preserved Emax in the simple model. Noncompetitive and irreversible are not synonyms.',3,[1490066300391,1490066305390,1490066311609]),
 (57,'chemical_antagonism','A drug that directly binds and neutralizes another substance produces {{c1::chemical antagonism}}.','This does not require competition for a cellular receptor; chelation is a useful example.',4,[1503672347649,1490066300391]),
 (58,'pk_antagonism','Induction of a drug-metabolizing enzyme can reduce another drug’s effect through {{c1::pharmacokinetic antagonism}}.','The target receptor need not change: less active drug reaches it.',4,[1503259126424,1565120487647]),
 (60,'window','The exposure range between useful and toxic effects is the {{c1::therapeutic window}}.','The therapeutic index is a ratio of median doses in a population; it does not by itself guarantee individual safety.',3,[1490066320396,1490066323578]),
 (65,'k_binders','Intestinal potassium binders lower serum K+ by increasing {{c1::fecal elimination}}.','Their action in the gut does not replace urgent membrane stabilization in life-threatening hyperkalemia.',4,[1532613551528,1472265265781]),
]
